{{block center|<math>\rho\frac{\partial u}{\partial t} = -\frac{\partial p}{\partial x} = -g\rho\frac{\partial \eta}{\partial x},</math> . . . . . (1)}}

where <math>\eta</math> denotes the surface-elevation at the point <math>x</math>. Again, the equation of continuity, viz.,

{{block center|<math>\frac{\partial u}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial v}{\partial y} = 0,</math> . . . . . . (2)}}

gives

{{block center|<math>v = -\int_0^y \frac{\partial u}{\partial x}dy = -y\frac{\partial u}{\partial x},</math> . . . . (3)}}

if the origin be taken at the bottom, the depth being assumed to be uniform. At the surface we have <math>y = h + \eta</math>, and <math>v = \partial n/\partial t</math>, subject to an error of the second order in the disturbance. To this degree of approximation we have then

{{block center|<math>\frac{\partial \eta}{\partial t} = -h\frac{\partial \eta}{\partial x}.</math> . . . . . (4)}}

If we eliminate <math>u</math> between (1) and (4) we obtain

{{block center|<math>\frac{\partial^2 \eta}{\partial t^2} = c^2\frac{\partial^2 \eta}{\partial x^2},</math> . . . . . . (5)}}

with

{{block center|<math>c^2 = gh.</math> . . . . . . . (6)}}

The solution is as in § 1, and represents two wave-systems travelling with the constant velocity <math>\sqrt{(gh)}</math>, which is that which would be acquired by a particle falling freely through a space equal to half the depth.

