Owing to its mathematical simplicity the theory of long waves in canals has been largely used to illustrate the dynamical theory of the tides. In the case of forced waves in a uniform canal, the equation (1) is replaced by
{{block center|<math>\frac{\partial u}{\partial t} = -g\frac{\partial \eta}{\partial x} + \text{X},</math> . . . . . (9)}}
where X represents the extraneous force. In the case of an equatorial canal surrounding the earth, the disturbing action of the moon, supposed (for simplicity) to revolve in a circular orbit in the plane of the equator, is represented by
{{block center|<math>\text{X} = -\frac{g\text{H}}{a}\sin 2\left(\sigma t + \frac{x}{a} + \epsilon\right),</math> . . . . (10)}}
where <math>a</math> is the earth's radius, H is the total range of the tide on the "equilibrium theory," and <math>\sigma</math> is the angular velocity of the moon relative to the rotating earth. The corresponding solution of the equations (4) and (9) is

