§ 4. ''Surface-Waves.''

This is the most familiar type of water-waves, but the theory is not altogether elementary. We will suppose in the first instance that the motion is in two dimensions <math>x</math>, <math>y</math>, horizontal and vertical respectively. The velocity-potential (see {{sc|Hydromechanics}}) must satisfy the equation
{{block center|<math>\frac{\partial^2\phi}{\partial x^2}+\frac{\partial^2\phi}{\partial y^2}=0,</math> . . . . . . . (1)}}
and must make <math>\partial\phi/\partial y=0</math> at the bottom, which is supposed to be plane and horizontal. The pressure-equation is, if we neglect the square of the velocity,
{{block center|<math>\frac{p}{\rho}=\frac{\partial\phi}{\partial t}-gy+\text{const}.</math> . . . . . . (2)}}
Hence, if the origin be taken in the undisturbed surface, we may write, for the surface-elevation,
